      "title": "Exile has not broken young Rohingya people. But survival cost us more than our homes | Ajas Khan",
      "content_html": "<p>Nine years growing up in a tent robbed me of my freedom and childhood. But my generation is determined to fight for a better future – one that we must be involved in shaping</p><p>Nine years ago, I was forced to flee my home in Maungdaw township, Rakhine state, Myanmar. I was 13 years old. I remember how quickly an average day turned into a day when life felt extinguished, leaving behind the place where I grew up with my family.</p><p>Even now, when I go to sleep, what comes into my mind is the memory of that journey, the fear of not knowing what awaited me. It took three days to reach refuge.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/sep/28/exile-has-not-broken-young-rohingya-people-but-survival-cost-us-more-than-our-homes\">Continue reading...</a>",
